{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>You shall not see the ox, etc.</b> This passage comes to hint in detail at the obligation of reproofs that the sons of El Chai, the righteous of the world, must administer to the people of HaShem. And these righteous ones are called brothers by virtue of their excellence, as I have alerted you that this appellation is the most exalted rank among all the epithets that may apply to the elite of the nation (Exodus Rabbah 52:5). And them HaShem commanded: if you see the ox — these are the sons of men who are likened to beasts, yet they are consecrated flock; therefore He called them ox and sheep. <b>Your brother</b> — this is the Holy One, blessed be He; and He attributed this name to Himself to indicate among which of His holy ones He is commanding, namely the righteous, as we have written." "And His saying <b>driven away</b> — after the manner of His saying (Deuteronomy 4:19) ״וְנִדַּחְתָּ וְהִשְׁתַּחֲוִיתָ לָהֶם״, etc., that one who transgresses the word of HaShem is called nidach (driven away). And HaShem charges that one must not ignore them but restore them to their brother — He is the eternal God. And He doubled the expression, saying ״הָשֵׁב תְּשִׁיבֵם״ — He intends that first one should restore them to the good path, and through this they will draw near to their Father in heaven; this is what is meant by ״תְּשִׁיבֵם לְאָחִיךָ״." "And His saying <b>and if your brother is not near, etc.</b> — by this He indicated that He is speaking of the time of the final exile, for he is not near, in the manner of His saying (Numbers 24:17) ״אֲשׁוּרֶנּוּ וְלֹא קָרוֹב״. Further, His saying ״וְלֹא יְדַעְתּוֹ״ — that the End is hidden and no one knows when the End of wonders will be; and this causes the distancing of hearts from faith and the wavering of footing, as our eyes see in these generations. Despite all this, HaShem commands: <b>and you shall gather it into the midst of your house</b> — this is the house of study; and teach him the paths of life and the way to dwell in light, so that he does not turn aside from the path and his enemy does not find him in false thoughts and specious arguments, for the light of Torah will save him. And this shall continue until HaShem is pleased and seeks him out — this is His saying <b>until your brother seeks it.</b> <b>And you shall restore it to him</b> — meaning, Scripture accounts it for him as though he restores it from being lost." "<b>Or</b> it further intends: that the sage must exert himself with the people of Israel through the power of Torah and moral instruction until the day of reckoning when HaShem seeks his soul, and then the spirit will return to Elohim. And the verse was precise in saying <b>and you shall restore it to him</b> — for if his ankles buckle the soul will not return to Elohim and will go to reproaches." "And His saying <b>and so you shall do.</b> For in the makeup of man there are three things: first, the physical constitution; second, the spiritual component within man; third, the portion of Torah that is betrothed to every one of Israel. Corresponding to the physical constitution He said <b>so you shall do for his donkey</b>; corresponding to the spiritual component He said <b>so you shall do for his garment</b> — for this aspect is called a garment, as they said in tractate Shabbat (152a-b): a parable to a king who distributed royal garments; and corresponding to the portion of Torah He said <b>and so you shall do for every lost thing of your brother</b>. And Scripture calls it a lost thing, as Rashbi hinted (Kiddushin 2a): a parable to one whose lost item was lost — who seeks whom, etc. For the portion that accrues to this man, behold it is lost from the world, since no one other than himself can attain it, as stated in the words of the mekubbalim; and through the act of restoring it in repentance, behold he finds it." "<b>And it further says</b> <b>you shall not see your brother's donkey, etc.</b> It intends by this that the righteous man should not say that he was commanded only concerning a man who has strayed through transgressions but is still capable of returning, but if he has fallen and multiplied his wickedness — for the fallen, can there be a rising, and hope lost from him? — it is not so. Rather, <b>you shall not see him</b> and ignore him; you must stand <b>with him</b>. He was precise in saying <b>with him</b> specifically — only if he assists in the matter of returning; but if he is falling and does not desire your lifting him up, you are exempt, for such a one enters the category of a scoffer, as it is written (Proverbs 9:8): ״אַל תּוֹכַח לֵץ פֶּן יִשְׂנָאֶךָּ״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :register {:flags {1 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:18:44.361250707Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}